<AddOnInstructionDefinitions Use="Context">
<AddOnInstructionDefinition Use="Target" Name="T_TtoISO_WkDate" Revision="1.0" RevisionExtension="00 Release" Vendor="Rockwell Automation" ExecutePrescan="false" ExecutePostscan="false" ExecuteEnableInFalse="false" CreatedDate="2014-01-20T16:31:07.551Z" CreatedBy="Not Available" EditedDate="2014-01-22T19:37:24.541Z"
 EditedBy="Not Available" SoftwareRevision="v18.02">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Date to ISO Week Date]]>
</Description>
<RevisionNote>
<![CDATA[Initial formal release]]>
</RevisionNote>
<AdditionalHelpText>
<![CDATA[This object converts a Date in common form (Year, Month, Day)
to an ISO-8601 Week Date (like 2014-W04-2, meaning Tuesday
in the fourth week of Week-Numbered Year 2014) for 2014-01-21.

This object calculates the ISO Year, ISO Week and ISO Day (day of the week).
Note the ISO Day is specified as 1=Monday .... 7=Sunday.  This object also
determines the number of weeks (52 or 53) in the calculated ISO Year.

The Date to convert is given in the Year, Month and Day of the
Ref_DT reference tag, of type DateTime (Y M D h m s us).

The Week-Numbered Year does not necessarily start on January 1.
It can start from December 29 through January 4.  The first week
of an ISO Week-Numbered Year is the week, beginning with Monday
and ending on Sunday, that contains the first THURSDAY of the
calendar year.  See Wikipedia "ISO Week Date" for more information.

